# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out native.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with core.autocrlf=true gets CRLF working files while the repo
# holds LF, and `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` fails locally on whitespace that does not
# exist in any commit — a failure that cannot be fixed, because there is nothing wrong to fix.
# It cost a debugging session: the formatter reported errors, running it "fixed" them, and git
# reported no change, because there had never been one. CI on Linux passed throughout.
#
# Declaring it here makes the repo's line endings a property of the repo rather than of whoever
# cloned it.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must keep LF wherever they are checked out — CRLF breaks the shebang.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the mirror case.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary, so never touched.
*.png binary
*.pfx binary
*.p12 binary
*.dump binary
